4. Architecture that emerges when treated as one system

I see seven interacting planes.

Plane	Function	Principal objects
Continuity	Durable identity and task state	StateVector, ledger, Golden Thread
Cognitive object	Typed reasoning and evidence	ThoughtObjects, claims, relations, provenance
Semantic logistics	Store and retrieve reusable structure	Quarry, Atlas, Router, Golden Horde
Instruction set	Define reusable transformations	Operators, Seals, daemons, interfaces
Control	Select and schedule reasoning	Metamap, Command Table, Formations
Governance	Control commitment and action	invariants, permissions, risk, reversibility
Consequence/learning	Test against reality and retain delta	experiments, afterglow, fitness, metabolism

The architecture is not really a strict hierarchy. It is a graph with typed cross-layer bridges. That is consistent with the repository’s own cross-layer doctrine.

The most coherent end-to-end interpretation is therefore:

Pantheon is a persistent cognitive blackboard whose contents are event-sourced ThoughtObjects; a planner compiles objectives into temporary operator graphs; interchangeable models and tools execute nodes; policy and evidence gates regulate state changes and external action; and an evaluation layer updates routing and reusable operator specifications.

That statement is derived, but it explains more of the corpus with fewer assumptions than the alternatives.

